---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GitHubLink from '../../../components/GitHubLink.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CSS styles chain · HCI Nerdz"
  description="You click part of a page, the style list crosses out the line that looks right, and nothing points at the rule that actually won."
  active="demos"
>
  <main class="shell page desk-page css-styles-chain-home">
    <nav class="identity-strip" aria-label="Demo identity">
      <a href="/">HCI Nerdz</a>
      <span class="identity-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/demos/">Demos</a>
      <span class="identity-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">CSS styles chain</span>
    </nav>
    <p class="vcs-row">
      <GitHubLink href="https://github.com/HCI-Nerdz/HCI-Nerdz.github.io" showStars={false} />
    </p>

    <header class="desk-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Demo · CSS styles chain</p>
      <h1>CSS styles chain</h1>
      <div class="intro">
        <p>
          You click part of a page and open the browser’s style list to find the setting that is
          actually in force. A lot of the lines are crossed out. The crossed-out line is often the
          one that says what you were looking for.
        </p>
        <p>
          Scroll, and the next copy of that same setting is crossed out too. The docs say the winner
          sits above the strike. On a real card, both background lines are struck, and the line that
          paints the card is further down, on a rule whose name does not look like the thing you
          clicked.
        </p>
        <p>
          The struck line does not point at the line that beat it. If that one lost as well, there
          is still no trail. You start to wonder if the line is crossed out because it uses a
          variable defined further up the page. That is a bad way to draw it, and it isn’t what
          happened. The variable is only the value. Another rule won.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rule’s name makes the hunt worse.
          <code>.doc .openblock.cards .exampleblock.tile &gt; .content</code>
          reads as if the box you clicked carried every one of those classes, or as if each class
          donated a property. The box only has <code>content</code>. The other words are checks on
          the boxes around it, and every check has to pass. A comma starts a second check. The grey
          half is the check that failed. Those grey words are not classes on your element. Hover the
          rule and the panel shows <code>(0, 6, 0)</code>. The middle number is how many classes
          are in the check. The panel never says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          A color or a font that came from the page around the box is dropped into the same flat
          list, as if the box had set it. Right-click a property and the last item in the menu, with
          no icon, is “View computed value.” That opens another tab, leaves a diagram of the box up,
          and then lists every name that contains the word you asked about. Ask about
          <code>background</code> and <code>background-color</code>, <code>background-image</code>,
          and the rest show up at the same size.
        </p>
        <p>
          The buttons below open another way of reading that same card. One is the tool. One is the
          card by itself, so you can open Chrome’s panel on it and compare. The third draws an
          earlier version of the idea inside a page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="launch-row">
      <a class="launch primary" data-window="css-styles-chain-inspector" href="/demos/css-styles-chain/inspect/" target="_blank">Open the inspector</a>
      <a class="launch" data-window="css-styles-chain-sample" href="/demos/css-styles-chain/sample.html" target="_blank">Open the sample page</a>
      <a class="launch" href="/demos/css-styles-chain/embedded/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">In-page layout</a>
    </div>
    <p id="launch-note" role="status"></p>

    <aside class="lesson">
      <h2>What the long selector is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>One test, read from the outside in.</strong>
          <code>.doc .openblock.cards .exampleblock.tile &gt; .content</code>
          matches only if every piece matches. The div you clicked has class
          <code>content</code>. <code>tile</code> and <code>exampleblock</code> are on its parent.
          <code>openblock</code> and <code>cards</code> are on a further ancestor.
          <code>doc</code> is further still. It does not mean the div carries all of those classes,
          and one class on the div is not enough.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grey names after a comma are a failed alternative.</strong>
          <code>.doc .exampleblock &gt; .content, .doc details.result &gt; .content</code>
          is two selectors. The comma means “or”. DevTools greys
          <code>.doc details.result &gt; .content</code> because this div is not inside
          <code>details.result</code>. Those grey tokens are not classes on your element.
          This inspector omits the half that missed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>(0, 6, 0) is six classes.</strong>
          The hover triple is (ids, classes, element names).
          <code>.content</code> is a class, so it counts in the middle number.
          The <code>&gt;</code> adds nothing. DevTools prints the triple and does not say that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The nest is two trees drawn as one list.</strong>
          Ancestry is a tree. Each property is a short stack of declarations that matched.
          DevTools flattens both into selector cards, including rules that matched an ancestor
          and alternatives that matched nothing. The chain keeps the stack, and the element
          tree keeps the ancestry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The chain is a number, not the selector.</strong>
          <code>class 6</code> is one stylesheet rule, numbered in the order the page loads its files.
          Opening the number inserts that one rule under the property. The triangle at the
          end of the line is the only control that opens every class. The cards are indented by how
          far the selector reaches into the element tree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The property you asked about stays the subject.</strong>
          In DevTools, “View computed value” sits last in the right-click menu and has no icon.
          It opens Computed, leaves the box-model diagram up, and filters by that word. Asking about
          <code>background</code> then lists every <code>background-*</code> name at the same weight.
          Here, names that only share the word sit under that property, in grey, after the chain.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .css-styles-chain-home {
    max-width: 72rem;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .identity-strip {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .identity-strip a {
    color: var(--paper-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .identity-strip a:hover {
    color: var(--focus);
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .identity-sep {
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .vcs-row {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .intro {
    max-width: 40rem;
    color: var(--paper-muted);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .intro p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .launch-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .launch {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 650;
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.9rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid var(--paper);
    color: var(--paper);
    background: transparent;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .launch.primary {
    background: var(--focus);
    border-color: var(--focus);
    color: #1c1915;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .launch:hover {
    border-color: var(--focus);
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .launch-note,
  .css-styles-chain-home #launch-note {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home #launch-note:empty {
    display: none;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .lesson {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .css-styles-chain-home .lesson li + li {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const row = document.querySelector('.css-styles-chain-home .launch-row');
  const note = document.querySelector('#launch-note');
  row?.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    const link = (event.target as Element | null)?.closest('a[data-window]');
    if (!(link instanceof HTMLAnchorElement)) return;
    event.preventDefault();
    const opened = window.open(link.href, link.dataset.window || '_blank', 'popup=yes,width=1200,height=840');
    if (!opened) {
      if (note) note.textContent = 'The browser blocked the window. Allow popups for this site, then click again.';
      return;
    }
    if (note) note.textContent = '';
    opened.focus();
  });
</script>
